# frozen_string_literal: true

# Generated by shipshape. This file is yours now — edit it, and shipshape will not
# overwrite it.
#
# Holds `a-permission-is-the-class-name`.
#
# **The permission IS the class name, not a transform of it.** Every transform is lossy and a
# lossy transform collides: `FooBar` and `Foo::Bar` both underscore to `:foo_bar`, so a grant
# issued for one runs the other. The constant name is injective, so uniqueness needs no guard.
#
# Renaming an operation renames the act, and stored grants are a data migration. That is the
# cost a separate constant hides rather than avoids.
module Permission
  # Every permission this application recognises, read off the classes.
  #
  #   Permission.catalogue(Command, Query, IoCommand, IoQuery, Workflow)
  #
  # **Eager-load first outside production**, or `descendants` answers only what has been
  # autoloaded and the catalogue comes back short — a coverage check passing while covering
  # nothing:
  #
  #   Rails.application.eager_load!
  #   missing = Permission.catalogue(Command, Query, Workflow) - CapabilityRecord.permissions
  #   raise "not in any capability: #{missing.join(', ')}" if missing.any?
  #
  # An operation in no capability is unreachable: fail-closed, correct, and invisible until it
  # surfaces as a refusal nobody can explain.
  #
  # Anonymous operations are left out, because they are never granted. So are nameless classes:
  # a `Class.new` subclass has no constant and so no permission.
  def self.catalogue(*doors)
    doors.flat_map { |door| door.descendants }
         .select(&:name)
         .reject(&:anonymous?)
         .flat_map { |operation| operation.respond_to?(:permissions) ? operation.permissions : [operation.permission] }
         .uniq
         .sort
  end

  def permission
    name.to_sym
  end

  # **`false` is load-bearing: this class only, never an ancestor or a module.**
  # `method_defined?` searches both, so a concern defining `anonymous_call` made every command
  # that included it public. Inheriting one from a parent operation is the same fail-open, and
  # is refused separately by `an-operation-is-a-leaf` — this holds the module half, which
  # nothing else does.
  def anonymous?
    instance_methods(false).include?(:anonymous_call) ||
      private_instance_methods(false).include?(:anonymous_call)
  end

  # **An operation demands its own permission and every permission it reaches** — a workflow
  # calling its steps and a command calling a query are one rule, not two.
  #
  # Without it each door still checked on its own way in, so an inner query refused mid-command
  # raised *there*: a 500 after the outer check passed, where the model promises a Result.
  #
  # `seen` breaks a cycle: two operations that reach each other would recurse until the stack
  # ends, and a stack overflow at boot is a worse way to learn about a cycle than the graph is.
  def permissions(seen = [])
    return aggregate(seen) unless seen.empty?

    @permissions ||= aggregate([])
  end

  # An anonymous operation contributes no permission of its own — it is never granted, so
  # demanding its name would forbid every caller — but what it reaches still travels upward.
  #
  # **Anonymity must be closed downward**: an `anonymous_call` reaching a guarded operation runs
  # that operation for nobody. `Shipshape/AnonymityIsClosedDownward` fails the build on it. This
  # does not raise, because a catalogue that died on one bad declaration would report none of
  # the good rows; `CallGraph.leaks` is the same fact as data.
  def aggregate(seen)
    return [] if seen.include?(self)

    onward = seen + [self]
    inherited = reaches.flat_map { |operation| operation.permissions(onward) }

    (anonymous? ? inherited : [permission] + inherited).uniq
  end

  def reaches
    Calls.in(self, anonymous? ? :anonymous_call : :call)
  end

  # **`permissions` is what the work needs; this is whether the door refuses.** An anonymous
  # operation aggregates what it reaches so a caller can demand it, and refuses nobody itself.
  #
  # **Private, because the only reason to ask is to branch**, and a decision has one home. A
  # page offers the action and places the refusal, or is handed a shape that already says what
  # is offerable.
  #
  # **The nil check comes first.** Behind the empty-demand check it let a caller that forgot
  # `actor:` through in silence.
  def permits?(actor)
    return true if anonymous?
    raise ArgumentError, "#{name} requires an actor" if actor.nil?

    demanded = permissions
    return true if demanded.empty?

    demanded.all? { |granted| actor.may?(granted) }
  end
  private :permits?

  # A query says what it found or raises, so a refusal cannot come back as a value without
  # every caller unwrapping one.
  class Refused < StandardError
    def initialize(permission)
      super("not permitted: #{permission}")
    end
  end
end
